<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Generalitat of Catalonia (1931-1939)</h1><p class="st-meta">1931 CE – 1939 CE · capital Barcelona</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Catalonia's autonomous government under the Second Republic, restored after two centuries and abolished after Franco's victory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 14 April 1931 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesc-macia">Francesc Macià</a> of the Republican Left of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catalan-republic-1641">Catalan Republic</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. After negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> it became an autonomous government that took the medieval name of the Generalitat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-autonomy-of-catalonia-1932">Statute of Autonomy</a> was approved in 1932.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Generalitat had its own parliament and powers over education, culture, public order and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, and promoted the Catalan language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lluis-companys">Lluís Companys</a> succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesc-macia">Macià</a> in 1933. In October 1934 Companys proclaimed a Catalan State within a federal republic, leading to his arrest and the statute's suspension until 1936. During the civil war it ran much of the Republican war effort in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nationalist forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in January 1939, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Franco</a> abolished the autonomy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lluis-companys">Companys</a> was handed over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> and executed in 1940. The Generalitat survived in exile and was restored in 1977.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1931 CE – 1939 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:98.13%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1931 CE – 1939 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Barcelona</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francesc Macià, Lluís Companys</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Catalan, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Catalan Generalitat, Autonomous Catalonia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=generalitat-of-catalonia-1931-1939" aria-label="Generalitat of Catalonia (1931-1939) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4733 702 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Generalitat of Catalonia (1931-1939) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5053 958L4909 989M5053 958L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4909 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5053 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5053 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=generalitat-of-catalonia-1931-1939">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

